!!! Dates and times, from milliseconds since the epoch.
!!!
!!! Three formats are needed and none of them is available: HTTP's own date
!!! format for Last-Modified, Date and Expires; the Common Log Format stamp an
!!! access log line carries; and an ISO instant for an error log. nil.std has
!!! a datetime module but nothing that turns an epoch millisecond count into
!!! a calendar date, which is the part that is actually hard.
!!!
!!! Everything here is UTC. HTTP requires GMT in a date header, and an access
!!! log in UTC is one that can be read alongside another machine's without
!!! anyone working out what two offsets meant on the night the clocks changed.
!!!
!!! The calendar arithmetic is Howard Hinnant's `civil_from_days`, which is
!!! exact integer work with no table and no leap-year special cases beyond the
!!! era arithmetic. It is written out rather than approximated because a date
!!! that is wrong one day in four years is worse than no date from-index all: it looks
!!! right in every test anyone writes in the other three.

book nilweb.time.clock

read nil.std.text
read nil.std.text.string_utils


constant ms-per-second as number is 1000
constant seconds-per-day as number is 86400


public calendar-time begin record
    field year as number
    !!! 1..12 and 1..31, as a person writes them, not zero-based.
    field month as number
    field day as number
    field hour as number
    field minute as number
    field second as number
    !!! 0 is Sunday, as every date format here spells it.
    field weekday as number
end record


!!! --- Break an epoch millisecond count into a UTC calendar time.
!!!
!!! A negative count — a file stamped before 1970, which happens on restored
!!! archives — is clamped to the epoch rather than formatted. The alternative
!!! is floor division over negative numbers, and Matrix's `over` truncates
!!! toward zero, so the arithmetic below would be a day out for exactly the
!!! inputs nobody tests. A clamped date is visibly wrong; an off-by-one date
!!! is not.

public civil-from-ms receives at-ms as number returns calendar-time begin paragraph
    value safe-ms is when at-ms is less than 0 give 0 otherwise give at-ms end when
    value total-seconds is safe-ms over ms-per-second
    value days is total-seconds over seconds-per-day
    value rest is total-seconds minus (days times seconds-per-day)

    !!! Days offset-days so the era arithmetic starts from 0000-03-01, which is
    !!! what makes the leap day the last day of a year rather than a hole in
    !!! the middle of one.
    value offset-days is days plus 719468
    value era is offset-days over 146097
    value day-of-era is offset-days minus (era times 146097)
    value year-of-era is (day-of-era minus (day-of-era over 1460)
        plus (day-of-era over 36524) minus (day-of-era over 146096)) over 365
    value day-of-year is day-of-era minus ((365 times year-of-era)
        plus (year-of-era over 4) minus (year-of-era over 100))
    value shifted-month is ((5 times day-of-year) plus 2) over 153
    value day is day-of-year minus (((153 times shifted-month) plus 2) over 5) plus 1
    value month is when shifted-month is less than 10
        give shifted-month plus 3
        otherwise give shifted-month minus 9
    end when
    value year is year-of-era plus (era times 400) plus (when month is at most 2 give 1 otherwise give 0 end when)

    give build calendar-time with
        year being year,
        month being month,
        day being day,
        hour being rest over 3600,
        minute being (rest over 60) remainder 60,
        second being rest remainder 60,
        weekday being (days plus 4) remainder 7
end paragraph


!!! --- An HTTP date, as RFC 9110 requires it.
!!!
!!! "Sun, 06 Nov 1994 08:49:37 GMT" — the IMF-fixdate form, fixed width, with
!!! English day and month abbreviations that do not vary by locale because the
!!! specification says they do not. The two obsolete formats a client may
!!! still send are read by `ms-from-http-date` and never written.

public http-date receives at-ms as number returns text begin paragraph
    value when-at is civil-from-ms with at-ms being at-ms
    give (weekday-name with index being when-at.weekday) plus ", "
        plus (two-digits with amount being when-at.day) plus " "
        plus (month-name with index being when-at.month) plus " "
        plus (four-digits with amount being when-at.year) plus " "
        plus (two-digits with amount being when-at.hour) plus ":"
        plus (two-digits with amount being when-at.minute) plus ":"
        plus (two-digits with amount being when-at.second) plus " GMT"
end paragraph


!!! --- The stamp in a Common Log Format line.
!!!
!!! "[10/Oct/2000:13:55:36 +0000]" — the form Apache and nginx write and every
!!! log reader already parses. The offset is always +0000 because everything
!!! here is UTC; a reader that expects a local offset gets a correct one.

public log-date receives at-ms as number returns text begin paragraph
    value when-at is civil-from-ms with at-ms being at-ms
    give (two-digits with amount being when-at.day) plus "/"
        plus (month-name with index being when-at.month) plus "/"
        plus (four-digits with amount being when-at.year) plus ":"
        plus (two-digits with amount being when-at.hour) plus ":"
        plus (two-digits with amount being when-at.minute) plus ":"
        plus (two-digits with amount being when-at.second) plus " +0000"
end paragraph


!!! --- An ISO 8601 instant, for an error log or a diagnostic.
!!! "1994-11-06T08:49:37Z" — sorts lexicographically in time order, which is
!!! the property a log line wants above all others.

public iso-instant receives at-ms as number returns text begin paragraph
    value when-at is civil-from-ms with at-ms being at-ms
    give (four-digits with amount being when-at.year) plus "-"
        plus (two-digits with amount being when-at.month) plus "-"
        plus (two-digits with amount being when-at.day) plus "T"
        plus (two-digits with amount being when-at.hour) plus ":"
        plus (two-digits with amount being when-at.minute) plus ":"
        plus (two-digits with amount being when-at.second) plus "Z"
end paragraph


!!! --- Read a date a client sent, as milliseconds, or minus one.
!!!
!!! Only the IMF-fixdate form is accepted. RFC 9110 lists two obsolete formats
!!! a recipient "must accept", and this does not: a conditional request whose
!!! date cannot be read is answered with the full representation, which is
!!! always correct and merely less efficient. Mis-reading a date is not —
!!! a date read as earlier than it was serves a stale response.
!!!
!!! A date that does not parse, or that names a month or day outside its
!!! range, is minus one so a caller cannot mistake it for the epoch.

public ms-from-http-date receives stated as text returns number begin paragraph
    value trimmed is nil.std.text::trim with s being stated
    !!! "Sun, 06 Nov 1994 08:49:37 GMT" is 29 characters, always.
    when not ((nil.std.text::length with s being trimmed) equals 29) then
        give 0 minus 1
    end when
    when not (nil.std.text::ends_with with s being trimmed, suffix being " GMT") then
        give 0 minus 1
    end when

    value day is two-digit-value with text-held being trimmed, from-index being 5
    value month is month-index with name being
        (nil.std.text::substring with s being trimmed, start being 8, length being 3)
    value year is four-digit-value with text-held being trimmed, from-index being 12
    value hour is two-digit-value with text-held being trimmed, from-index being 17
    value minute is two-digit-value with text-held being trimmed, from-index being 20
    value second is two-digit-value with text-held being trimmed, from-index being 23

    value sane is day is at least 1 and day is at most 31
        and month is at least 1 and year is at least 1970
        and hour is at least 0 and hour is at most 23
        and minute is at least 0 and minute is at most 59
        and second is at least 0 and second is at most 60
    when not sane then
        give 0 minus 1
    end when
    give ms-from-civil with year being year, month being month, day being day,
        hour being hour, minute being minute, second being second
end paragraph


!!! --- The inverse of civil-from-ms, for a date that was read rather than
!!! formatted. Hinnant's `days_from_civil`, the same arithmetic run backwards.

public ms-from-civil receives year as number, month as number, day as number,
    hour as number, minute as number, second as number returns number begin paragraph
    value shifted-year is year minus (when month is at most 2 give 1 otherwise give 0 end when)
    value era is shifted-year over 400
    value year-of-era is shifted-year minus (era times 400)
    value shifted-month is when month is greater than 2 give month minus 3 otherwise give month plus 9 end when
    value day-of-year is (((153 times shifted-month) plus 2) over 5) plus day minus 1
    value day-of-era is (365 times year-of-era) plus (year-of-era over 4)
        minus (year-of-era over 100) plus day-of-year
    value days is (era times 146097) plus day-of-era minus 719468
    give (((days times seconds-per-day) plus (hour times 3600) plus (minute times 60) plus second)
        times ms-per-second)
end paragraph


weekday-name receives index as number returns text begin paragraph
    give switch index
        case 0 then stop switching with "Sun"
        case 1 then stop switching with "Mon"
        case 2 then stop switching with "Tue"
        case 3 then stop switching with "Wed"
        case 4 then stop switching with "Thu"
        case 5 then stop switching with "Fri"
        otherwise stop switching with "Sat"
    end switch
end paragraph


month-name receives index as number returns text begin paragraph
    give switch index
        case 1 then stop switching with "Jan"
        case 2 then stop switching with "Feb"
        case 3 then stop switching with "Mar"
        case 4 then stop switching with "Apr"
        case 5 then stop switching with "May"
        case 6 then stop switching with "Jun"
        case 7 then stop switching with "Jul"
        case 8 then stop switching with "Aug"
        case 9 then stop switching with "Sep"
        case 10 then stop switching with "Oct"
        case 11 then stop switching with "Nov"
        otherwise stop switching with "Dec"
    end switch
end paragraph


month-index receives name as text returns number begin paragraph
    give switch name
        case "Jan" then stop switching with 1
        case "Feb" then stop switching with 2
        case "Mar" then stop switching with 3
        case "Apr" then stop switching with 4
        case "May" then stop switching with 5
        case "Jun" then stop switching with 6
        case "Jul" then stop switching with 7
        case "Aug" then stop switching with 8
        case "Sep" then stop switching with 9
        case "Oct" then stop switching with 10
        case "Nov" then stop switching with 11
        case "Dec" then stop switching with 12
        otherwise stop switching with 0 minus 1
    end switch
end paragraph


!!! --- Fixed-width digits. A date header is fixed width by specification, and
!!! a caller must not have to remember to pad.

public two-digits receives amount as number returns text begin paragraph
    when amount is less than 0 or amount is greater than 99 then
        give "00"
    end when
    give (digit-glyph with amount being amount over 10) plus (digit-glyph with amount being amount remainder 10)
end paragraph


four-digits receives amount as number returns text begin paragraph
    when amount is less than 0 or amount is greater than 9999 then
        give "0000"
    end when
    give (digit-glyph with amount being amount over 1000)
        plus (digit-glyph with amount being (amount over 100) remainder 10)
        plus (digit-glyph with amount being (amount over 10) remainder 10)
        plus (digit-glyph with amount being amount remainder 10)
end paragraph


digit-glyph receives amount as number returns text begin paragraph
    give switch amount
        case 0 then stop switching with "0"
        case 1 then stop switching with "1"
        case 2 then stop switching with "2"
        case 3 then stop switching with "3"
        case 4 then stop switching with "4"
        case 5 then stop switching with "5"
        case 6 then stop switching with "6"
        case 7 then stop switching with "7"
        case 8 then stop switching with "8"
        otherwise stop switching with "9"
    end switch
end paragraph


two-digit-value receives text-held as text, from-index as number returns number begin paragraph
    value high is digit-value with text-held being text-held, from-index being from-index
    value low is digit-value with text-held being text-held, from-index being from-index plus 1
    when high is less than 0 or low is less than 0 then
        give 0 minus 1
    end when
    give (high times 10) plus low
end paragraph


four-digit-value receives text-held as text, from-index as number returns number begin paragraph
    value a is digit-value with text-held being text-held, from-index being from-index
    value b is digit-value with text-held being text-held, from-index being from-index plus 1
    value c is digit-value with text-held being text-held, from-index being from-index plus 2
    value d is digit-value with text-held being text-held, from-index being from-index plus 3
    when a is less than 0 or b is less than 0 or c is less than 0 or d is less than 0 then
        give 0 minus 1
    end when
    give (a times 1000) plus (b times 100) plus (c times 10) plus d
end paragraph


digit-value receives text-held as text, from-index as number returns number begin paragraph
    value code is nil.std.text::byte_at with s being text-held, index being from-index
    when code is less than 48 or code is greater than 57 then
        give 0 minus 1
    end when
    give code minus 48
end paragraph
